1
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:01,903
Ed:

2
00:00:01,903 --> 00:00:05,284
Did you make notes while you were watching the performances of Romeo

3
00:00:05,284 --> 00:00:06,310
and Juliet, Gemma?

4
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:06,732
Gemma:

5
00:00:06,732 --> 00:00:07,436
Yes, I did.

6
00:00:07,436 --> 00:00:09,604
I found it quite hard though.

7
00:00:09,604 --> 00:00:12,240
I kept getting too involved in the play.

8
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:12,547
Ed:

9
00:00:12,547 --> 00:00:13,161
Me too.

10
00:00:13,161 --> 00:00:15,002
I ended up not taking notes.

11
00:00:15,002 --> 00:00:17,764
I wrote down my impressions when I got home.

12
00:00:17,764 --> 00:00:21,140
Do you mind if I check a few things with you?

13
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:22,981
In case I’ve missed anything.

14
00:00:22,981 --> 00:00:26,050
And I’ve also got some questions about our assignment.

15
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:26,377
Gemma:

16
00:00:26,377 --> 00:00:28,990
No, it’s good to talk things through.

17
00:00:28,990 --> 00:00:30,950
I may have missed things too.

18
00:00:30,950 --> 00:00:31,302
Ed:

19
00:00:31,302 --> 00:00:32,006
OK great.

20
00:00:32,006 --> 00:00:37,286
So first of all, I’m not sure how much information we should include in

21
00:00:37,286 --> 00:00:37,990
our reviews.

22
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:38,362
Gemma:

23
00:00:38,362 --> 00:00:38,735
Right.

24
00:00:38,735 --> 00:00:42,830
Well, I don’t think we need to describe what happens.

25
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:47,670
Especially as Romeo and Juliet is one of Shakespeare’s most well-known plays.

26
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:48,024
Ed:

27
00:00:48,024 --> 00:00:49,797
Yeah, everyone knows the story.

28
00:00:49,797 --> 00:00:55,113
In an essay we’d focus on the poetry and Shakespeare’s use of imagery

29
00:00:55,113 --> 00:00:55,467
etc.,

30
00:00:55,467 --> 00:00:58,657
but that isn’t really relevant in a review.

31
00:00:58,657 --> 00:01:02,910
We’re supposed to focus on how effective this particular production is.

32
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:03,282
Gemma:

33
00:01:03,282 --> 00:01:03,653
Mmm.

34
00:01:03,653 --> 00:01:07,740
We should say what made it a success or a failure.

35
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:08,033
Ed:

36
00:01:08,033 --> 00:01:12,140
And part of that means talking about the emotional impact the performance had on

37
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:12,433
us.

38
00:01:12,433 --> 00:01:13,900
I think that’s important.

39
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:14,315
Gemma:

40
00:01:14,315 --> 00:01:14,730
Yes.

41
00:01:14,730 --> 00:01:20,537
And we should definitely mention how well the director handled important bits of the

42
00:01:20,537 --> 00:01:24,270
play – like when Romeo climbs onto Juliet’s balcony.

43
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:24,570
Ed:

44
00:01:24,570 --> 00:01:26,670
And the fight between Mercutio and Tybalt.

45
00:01:26,670 --> 00:01:27,001
Gemma:

46
00:01:27,001 --> 00:01:27,333
Yes.

47
00:01:27,333 --> 00:01:32,634
It would also be interesting to mention the theatre space and how the director used it

48
00:01:32,634 --> 00:01:36,610
but I don’t think we’ll have space in 800 words.

49
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:37,521
Ed:

50
00:01:37,521 --> 00:01:38,432
No.

51
00:01:38,432 --> 00:01:39,344
OK.

52
00:01:39,344 --> 00:01:43,900
That all sounds quite straightforward.

53
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:44,231
Ed:

54
00:01:44,231 --> 00:01:47,870
So what about The Emporium Theatre’s production of the play?

55
00:01:47,870 --> 00:01:48,217
Gemma:

56
00:01:48,217 --> 00:01:52,730
I thought some things worked really well but there were some problems too.

57
00:01:52,730 --> 00:01:53,140
Ed:

58
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:53,550
Yeah.

59
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:56,010
What about the set, for example?

60
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:56,363
Gemma:

61
00:01:56,363 --> 00:01:58,833
I think it was visually really stunning.

62
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:03,420
I’d say that was probably the most memorable thing about this production.

63
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:03,783
Ed:

64
00:02:03,783 --> 00:02:04,873
You’re right.

65
00:02:04,873 --> 00:02:07,053
The set design was really amazing,

66
00:02:07,053 --> 00:02:11,050
but actually I have seen similar ideas used in other productions.

67
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:11,414
Gemma:

68
00:02:11,414 --> 00:02:12,868
What about the lighting?

69
00:02:12,868 --> 00:02:17,960
Some of the scenes were so dimly lit it was quite hard to see.

70
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:18,290
Ed:

71
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:19,940
I didn’t dislike it.

72
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:23,240
It helped to change the mood of the quieter scenes.

73
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:23,502
Gemma:

74
00:02:23,502 --> 00:02:24,810
That’s a good point.

75
00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:25,070
Ed:

76
00:02:25,070 --> 00:02:26,890
What did you think of the costumes?

77
00:02:26,890 --> 00:02:27,184
Gemma:

78
00:02:27,184 --> 00:02:30,710
I was a bit surprised by the contemporary dress, I must say.

79
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:30,991
Ed:

80
00:02:30,991 --> 00:02:32,674
Yeah – I think it worked well,

81
00:02:32,674 --> 00:02:35,200
but I had assumed it would be more conventional.

82
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:35,534
Gemma:

83
00:02:35,534 --> 00:02:36,202
Me too.

84
00:02:36,202 --> 00:02:40,881
I liked the music at the beginning and I thought the musicians were brilliant,

85
00:02:40,881 --> 00:02:46,562
but I thought they were wasted because the music didn’t have much impact in Acts 2

86
00:02:46,562 --> 00:02:47,230
and 3.

87
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:47,540
Ed:

88
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:49,090
Yes – that was a shame.

89
00:02:49,090 --> 00:02:49,396
Gemma:

90
00:02:49,396 --> 00:02:53,992
One problem with this production was that the actors didn’t deliver the lines that

91
00:02:53,992 --> 00:02:54,298
well.

92
00:02:54,298 --> 00:02:55,830
They were speaking too fast.

93
00:02:55,830 --> 00:02:56,112
Ed:

94
00:02:56,112 --> 00:02:57,801
It was a problem I agree,

95
00:02:57,801 --> 00:03:01,742
but I thought it was because they weren’t speaking loudly enough – especially at

96
00:03:01,742 --> 00:03:03,150
key points in the play.

97
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:03,448
Gemma:

98
00:03:03,448 --> 00:03:06,130
I actually didn’t have a problem with that.

99
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:06,433
Ed:

100
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:09,765
It’s been an interesting experience watching different versions of Romeo

101
00:03:09,765 --> 00:03:11,280
and Juliet, hasn’t it?

102
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:11,622
Gemma:

103
00:03:11,622 --> 00:03:11,963
Definitely.

104
00:03:11,963 --> 00:03:15,720
It’s made me realise how relevant the play still is.

105
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:16,053
Ed:

106
00:03:16,053 --> 00:03:16,385
Right.

107
00:03:16,385 --> 00:03:19,712
I mean a lot’s changed since Shakespeare’s time,

108
00:03:19,712 --> 00:03:22,041
but in many ways nothing’s changed.

109
00:03:22,041 --> 00:03:25,700
There are always disagreements and tension between teenagers and their parents.

110
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:26,053
Gemma:

111
00:03:26,053 --> 00:03:26,405
Yes,

112
00:03:26,405 --> 00:03:30,991
that’s something all young people can relate to – more than the violence

113
00:03:30,991 --> 00:03:33,460
and the extreme emotions in the play.

114
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:33,827
Ed:

115
00:03:33,827 --> 00:03:36,760
How did you find watching it in translation?

116
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:37,119
Gemma:

117
00:03:37,119 --> 00:03:37,838
Really interesting.

118
00:03:37,838 --> 00:03:40,354
I expected to find it more challenging,

119
00:03:40,354 --> 00:03:43,230
but I could follow the story pretty well.

120
00:03:43,230 --> 00:03:43,567
Ed:

121
00:03:43,567 --> 00:03:47,612
I stopped worrying about not being able to understand all the words

122
00:03:47,612 --> 00:03:49,635
and focused on the actors’ expressions.

123
00:03:49,635 --> 00:03:51,320
The ending was pretty powerful.

124
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:51,807
Gemma:

125
00:03:51,807 --> 00:03:52,293
Yes.

126
00:03:52,293 --> 00:03:55,700
That somehow intensified the emotion for me.

127
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:56,045
Ed:

128
00:03:56,045 --> 00:04:00,880
Did you know Shakespeare’s been translated into more languages than any other writer?

129
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:01,260
Gemma:

130
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:05,440
What’s the reason for his international appeal, do you think?

131
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:05,765
Ed:

132
00:04:05,765 --> 00:04:10,640
I was reading that it’s because his plays are about basic themes that people

133
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:11,940
everywhere are familiar with.

134
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:12,326
Gemma:

135
00:04:12,326 --> 00:04:12,711
Yeah,

136
00:04:12,711 --> 00:04:16,182
and they can also be understood on different levels.

137
00:04:16,182 --> 00:04:18,110
The characters have such depth.

138
00:04:18,110 --> 00:04:18,518
Ed:

139
00:04:18,518 --> 00:04:22,600
Right – which allows directors to experiment and find new angles.

140
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:23,100
Gemma:

141
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:25,600
That’s really important because …
